# APK update reconnect (Nov 2026)

After a self-update the tablet can sit on the Connecting panel until a forced reconnect. A half-open WebSocket looks alive, Hello is sent, status becomes CONNECTED, and no Welcome ever arrives — so the automatic backoff loop never runs.

## Symptom and UI

- Fresh process: `PlayerViewModel.isPaired` starts `false`, no main document. `CONNECTING` sets `InfoPanelState.Connecting`.
- `publish` marks `CONNECTED` as soon as Hello is sent, before any server reply. The `CONNECTED` branch leaves the info panel alone, so Connecting stays on screen.
- Welcome never arrives → stuck. Manual `reconnect()` opens a new socket and recovers.

## Ownership (`ConnectionRepository`)

- Each `connect` / `reconnect` owns the loop with a token. Every attempt has a `connectionId`.
- A replaced or disconnected attempt must not publish its session, overwrite status, or clear its replacement while closing.
- `connectionState` and `receivedMessages` are the only status/message streams. `trySend` can be scoped to one `connectionId`.
- Set owner and job before emitting flows: a `Main.immediate` collector can reenter `connect`/`reconnect` inline.

## Startup watchdog

- `HttpTimeout` (15 s) bounds only the HTTP upgrade. It does not bound an upgraded socket waiting for the first application reply.
- `CONNECTION_START_TIMEOUT_MS` (30 s) runs beside `webSocket { … }`. If no decoded `ServerMessage` arrives, throw and let the backoff loop replace the socket. Cancel the watchdog on the first reply (and in `finally`).
- Pass `delay(CONNECTION_START_TIMEOUT_MS.milliseconds)` — Duration, not the Long overload.
- WebSocket client pings (`NetworkModule`, 20 s) do not fix a peer that never answers Hello.

## Add-on

- `PlayerHub.serve` replaces `sessions[deviceId]` and closes the previous session. A clean new Hello should welcome; the hang is on the player when the upgraded socket stays silent.

## Verify

```bash
./gradlew :player:testDebugUnitTest --tests '*ConnectionRepositoryTest' :player:detekt
```
